kinds of possibilities. So had Johnson's faith, for instance, been wavering in the final months of his life? You know, was he worrying that he might go insane, might become a lunatic? Or, as you suggested, was it the memory of something sexual, perhaps, which had been causing him shame and making him worry that he might be damned? And Boswell doesn't discount any of these possibilities because he's been doing his research. And he knows that as a young man, actually, Johnson had had religious doubts. So David Garrick, the great writer, Boswell's oldest friend. He'd said Johnson did have religious doubts as a young man, we've seen how Johnson did indeed dread being plunged into madness. And also, it's evident even from things that Johnson said himself, often making a kind of jest out of it,
